  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $1.4B in contracts in FY2027 to date. The agency recorded 1,424 awards, with an average award size of $950K. Obligations fell 91.6% year over year. Historical annual obligations show larger totals in prior years: FY22 obligated $15.2B, FY23 obligated $16.1B, FY24 obligated $15B, FY25 obligated $15.8B, and FY26 obligated $16.1B. The current-year total is partial and reflects fiscal year to date reporting. Contracting activity in FY2027 to date concentrates in a few NAICS categories and vendors, with the largest single NAICS receiving $525M and the largest vendor receiving $215M. These figures describe the distribution and scale of NASA obligations through the current fiscal year to date and highlight a sharp year-over-year decline in obligations compared with the prior full fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has NASA contract spending changed year over year?",
        "body": "The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $1.4B in FY2027 to date, down 91.6% year over year. FY26 obligations were $16.1B, and prior full fiscal years show FY25 at $15.8B, FY24 at $15B, FY23 at $16.1B, and FY22 at $15.2B. The FY2027 total is a fiscal year to date figure, not a full-year total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors received the most NASA obligations in FY2027 to date?",
        "body": "Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) received $525M, the largest NAICS category. CALIFORNIA INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY received $215M, THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC received $132M, and BLUE ORIGIN, LLC received $99.2M among the top vendors. Other notable categories include Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $188M and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $179M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does National Aeronautics and Space Administration spend on contracts?",
      "a": "NASA obligated $1.4B in contracts in FY2027 to date. The agency recorded 1,424 awards with an average award size of $950K, and obligations fell 91.6% year over year. Historical full-year obligations were larger, for example FY26 obligated $16.1B and FY25 obligated $15.8B."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does National Aeronautics and Space Administration buy?",
      "a": "NASA buys a mix of research, transportation, manufacturing, and support services. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) received $525M, Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation received $188M, and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing received $179M in FY2027 to date."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are National Aeronautics and Space Administration's largest contractors?",
      "a": "NASA's largest contractors in FY2027 to date include CALIFORNIA INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY at $215M and THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC at $132M. BLUE ORIGIN, LLC received $99.2M, THE BOEING COMPANY received $91.3M, and additional top vendors include AEROJET ROCKETDYNE OF DE, INC at $53.4M and IMPULSE SPACE, INC. at $37.1M."
